Interview Tip

There are two common DFS patterns you'll encounter:

1. Preorder DFS (Most graph problems)
dfs(node){

    visited[node] = true;   // Process first

    for(neighbor){
        dfs(neighbor);
    }
}

Used in:

✅ Number of Islands
✅ Pacific Atlantic Water Flow
✅ Flood Fill
✅ Clone Graph
✅ Graph Traversal


2. Postorder DFS
dfs(node){

    for(neighbor){
        dfs(neighbor);
    }

    answer.add(node);       // Process after children
}

Used in:

✅ Topological Sort (DFS version)
✅ Course Schedule II
✅ Tree DP
✅ Serialize/Destroy tree problems


Quick rule to remember
| When is the current node processed? | Traversal Type                |
| ----------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| **Before** recursive calls          | ✅ Preorder                    |
| **After** recursive calls           | ✅ Postorder                   |
| Between left and right child        | ✅ Inorder (binary trees only) |


Does Inorder exist here?

❌ No.

Inorder is only defined for Binary Trees because there is a clear meaning of:

Left
Node
Right

A graph (or a grid) has:

Up
Down
Left
Right

or even an arbitrary number of neighbors.

There is no universal "middle" position, so Inorder doesn't apply.